[
  {"id": "question 1721", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 48.052 (and TR 21.905), a Base Transceiver Station (BTS) is 'a network component which serves one cell, and is controlled by a Base Station Controller'.", "source": "3GPP TS 48.052"},
  {"id": "question 1724", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TR 21.917 (R17) on slice support: 'In order to support service continuity for specific slice(s), the NG-RAN node may use Multi-Carrier Resource Sharing or Resource Repartitioning to allocate resources to a slice in case of slice resources shortage.'", "source": "3GPP TR 21.917"},
  {"id": "question 1770", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "Per TS 23.501 5.2.5, the NG-RAN may initiate Unified Access Control when AMFs request load restriction (via OVERLOAD START), or when requested by OAM, or when triggered by NG-RAN itself. So options 1, 2, and 3 are all valid triggers - none is uniquely correct.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.501 5.2.5"},
  {"id": "question 1975", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 36.410, 'Two types of S1 interfaces are thus defined at the boundary depending on the EPC access point: S1-MME towards an MME and S1-U towards an S-GW.' eNodeB connects to EPC via S1-MME (control) and S1-U (user plane).", "source": "3GPP TS 36.410"},
  {"id": "question 2007", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 32.295 and TS 32.240, 'The Ga is the reference point from the Charging Data Function (CDF) to the Charging Gateway Function (CGF), which is intended for the transport of Charging Data Records (CDRs).'", "source": "3GPP TS 32.295"},
  {"id": "question 2050", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 28.310 / TS 32.551 definitions: 'ES activation: procedure to power down a cell or network function for energy saving purposes.' (ES deactivation is power up.)", "source": "3GPP TS 28.310, TS 32.551"},
  {"id": "question 2131", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "In 3GPP the abbreviation NSC stands for 'Network Slice Customer' (a role, per TS 28.530), not a 'network slice catalogue'. The slice-template concept is the NST/NEST (TS 28.530, GSMA NG.116) but no option cleanly maps to a 'catalogue' role.", "source": "3GPP TS 28.530"},
  {"id": "question 2278", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 1, "evidence": "In EN-DC (NSA Option 3x), IMS signaling and IMS voice (VoLTE) are anchored on the LTE eNB (Master Node) using MCG bearer, not SCG bearer. The MN/eNB prevents the voice bearer (QCI1) from being configured as a split/SCG bearer; IMS signaling uses QCI5 default bearer on the LTE side.", "source": "3GPP TS 37.340, EN-DC voice guidelines"},
  {"id": "question 2355", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 38.401 8.4.3: 'The CU of SN sends the UE CONTEXT MODIFICATION REQUEST message to the DU of SN to suspend the SCG of the UE before the UE enters into RRC_INACTIVE state'. The CU (not the DU) initiates the suspension.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.401 8.4.3"},
  {"id": "question 2380", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per TS 23.501 5.8.2 and Cisco 5G-UPF docs: 'N4 session management procedures are used to control the functionality of the UPF.' SMF creates, updates, and removes N4 session contexts in UPF.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.501 5.8.2"},
  {"id": "question 2396", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "Per TS 23.501 4.3.1, BOTH PGW-C+SMF AND UPF+PGW-U are dedicated for 5GS-EPC interworking. Option 2 (UPF+PGW-U) is one valid pair, option 3 (PGW-C+SMF) is the other valid pair - the question asks 'which entities' (plural) and only one pair is listed per option.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.501 4.3.1"},
  {"id": "question 2408", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "Per TS 22.228, HSS provides multiple functions: user identification/numbering/addressing, user security info, user location info, and user profile. Options 2, 3, 4, and 5 each describe a valid HSS function; option 1 ('support call control servers') appears in older TS 22.228 phrasing but is not uniquely the HSS's primary function.", "source": "3GPP TS 22.228"},
  {"id": "question 2488", "verdict": "wrong", "correct_option": 4, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 37.213 / 38.300 for NR-U, the contention window CWp is defined per Channel Access Priority Class (CAPC) 1-4, and CW adjustment on HARQ-ACK feedback operates per priority class. CW adjustment is not done across all priority classes.", "source": "3GPP TS 37.213"},
  {"id": "question 2570", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per IEEE 802.3 (10BASE-T 802.3i, 100BASE-TX 802.3u), 10/100Base-T uses 2 pairs: pair 2 (orange, pins 1-2) for transmit and pair 3 (green, pins 3-6) for receive.", "source": "IEEE 802.3"},
  {"id": "question 2608", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 23.288 5.7.19 (PDU Session Traffic Analytics): 'This procedure is used by the NWDAF service consumer (e.g. PCF) to subscribe or request PDU Session Traffic analytics'.", "source": "3GPP TS 23.288 5.7.19"},
  {"id": "question 2640", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 38.331 and patents on UE capability transfer: 'the gNB always requests the UE UTRA-FDD capabilities before handover to UTRA-FDD' to avoid START values desynchronisation and key replaying.", "source": "3GPP TS 38.331"},
  {"id": "question 2673", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 3, "evidence": "Per IEEE 802.3 (Perle Ethernet Basics reference): 'The MAU, developed for twisted pair, is equipped with 4 data pins: 2 for sending, 2 for receiving. This is the basis for full duplex Ethernet.' The MAU provides the physical attachment that enables simultaneous TX/RX.", "source": "IEEE 802.3"},
  {"id": "question 2771", "verdict": "correct", "correct_option": 2, "evidence": "Per 3GPP TS 23.501 5.22.4 (QoS Mechanisms applied to established QoS Flows): 'SMF: Congestion management procedures in the SMF will provide priority to QoS Flows established for sessions during periods of extreme overload.'", "source": "3GPP TS 23.501 5.22.4"},
  {"id": "question 3060", "verdict": "ambiguous", "correct_option": null, "evidence": "MIB purpose is described variously in standards: RFC 1155 says 'MIB defines the structure for the storage of network information' (option 1), while RFC 1213/IBM describe it as 'a database containing the information pertinent to network management' (option 5). Both descriptions are standard, making the marked answer ambiguous vs option 1.", "source": "RFC 1155, RFC 1213"}
]